---
import Math from "../../../components/Math.astro";
import { timingOfBadLuck as study } from "../../../data/experiments";
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const title = "The timing of bad luck | Independent Reinforcement Learning Lab";
const description =
  "A controlled study of how event timing, step-size rules, and one-step TD backup depth determine whether one adverse outcome changes a finite-budget policy.";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonicalPath={study.slug}
  article
  published="2026-08-27"
  updated={study.updated}
>
  <article class="research-note">
    <header class="article-hero">
      <div class="article-meta">
        <span>{study.kind.toUpperCase()} {study.number}</span>
        <span>27 AUGUST 2026</span>
        <span>MECHANISM STUDY</span>
      </div>
      <h1>The timing <em>of bad luck.</em></h1>
      <p class="article-deck">{study.subtitle}</p>
      <p class="article-standfirst">
        A bad result can change what an agent samples next. This note isolates how
        the time of that result, the learning-rate rule, and the number of TD backups
        between outcome and choice determine whether the agent ends with the wrong policy.
      </p>
      <div class="article-byline">
        <span>Patrick Starvaggi</span>
        <span>8 minute read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <aside class="study-notice" aria-labelledby="study-status-title">
      <p id="study-status-title">RESULTS PUBLISHED · 27 AUG 2026</p>
      <div>
        <strong>One adverse terminal outcome produced a timing window whose shape reversed with the step-size rule and changed sharply with route depth.</strong>
        <span>Exact depth-one calculation; 20,000 paired seeds per simulated point; complete analysis in under 30 seconds.</span>
      </div>
    </aside>

    <section class="article-section" aria-labelledby="question-title">
      <div class="section-rail"><span>01</span><p>THE QUESTION</p></div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="question-title">Bad evidence changes future evidence.</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          A learner does not passively receive a fixed dataset. What it currently
          believes changes what it chooses, and therefore what it gets to observe next.
        </p>
        <p>
          An adverse result on a promising route can make that route non-greedy. If
          exploration is already declining, the agent may then stop collecting the
          observations required to correct the estimate. The broad phenomenon is known
          as the <em>hot-stove effect</em>; it was described in adaptive learning well
          before this note. <a href="https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/orsc.12.5.523.10092">Denrell and March</a>
          showed how bad samples can suppress the sampling that would undo an
          underestimate, and <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22924882/">Shteingart, Neiman, and Loewenstein</a>
          measured lasting first-outcome effects in human choice.
        </p>
        <p class="research-question"><strong>Question.</strong> Under a fixed training budget, when does one adverse terminal outcome have the greatest chance of changing the final greedy policy?</p>
        <p>
          The existence of path dependence is not the claim. The narrower object here
          is its timing profile, and how that profile changes when the estimator carries
          prior evidence differently and the outcome is several TD backups away from the choice.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="article-section" aria-labelledby="world-title">
      <div class="section-rail"><span>02</span><p>THE INTERVENTION</p></div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="world-title">One choice, one controlled shock.</h2>
        <p>
          At the start, the agent chooses between two routes. The safe route terminates
          immediately for <Math tex="s=0.9" />. The risky route enters a deterministic
          chain and ordinarily terminates for <Math tex="g=1" />. Its depth
          <Math tex="d\in\{1,3,6\}" /> counts the state-action values from the start
          decision through the terminal predecessor. There are no later choices, and
          <Math tex="\gamma=1" />. The risky route is therefore truly optimal at every depth.
        </p>
        <p>
          Before an event at training round <Math tex="\tau" />, I calibrate exactly
          <Math tex="n=\tau" /> successful risky traversals. The risky values equal one
          and their visit counts equal <Math tex="n" />. I then force the risky route
          once. The treatment receives <Math tex="b=-5" /> at the terminal transition;
          its paired control receives the ordinary reward one. Every later risky outcome
          is again one, and the two arms share the same future random numbers.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is an impulse response, not a simulation that waits for a rare event to
          occur naturally. If the bad reward arose with probability <Math tex="p" /> in
          a stationary reward distribution, the risky route would still have the higher
          mean whenever <Math tex="p<1/60" />. Here I condition on one event so its
          causal effect can be estimated without a giant rare-event sweep.
        </p>
      </div>
      <dl class="protocol-grid">
        <div><dt>Training horizon</dt><dd>200 route choices</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Exploration</dt><dd>ε: 0.40 → 0.01</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Step sizes</dt><dd>1 / N and α = 0.20</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Paired samples</dt><dd>20,000 per point</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The outcome is finite-budget policy error: the probability that the treated
          learner finishes safe-greedy while its paired ordinary-outcome control remains
          risky-greedy. “Lock-in” below always means this operational, finite-horizon event.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="article-section" aria-labelledby="exact-title">
      <div class="section-rail"><span>03</span><p>THE EXACT CASE</p></div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="exact-title">At depth one, recovery can be counted.</h2>
        <p>
          Suppose the adverse result follows <Math tex="n" /> ordinary risky outcomes
          and is followed by <Math tex="k" /> more. Under a sample-average update,
        </p>
        <Math display tex="\bar q_{n,k}=\frac{(n+k)g+b}{n+k+1}." />
        <p>The number of later risky visits required to make that action greedy again is</p>
        <Math display tex="k_{\mathrm{avg}}(n)=\max\!\left(0,\left\lfloor\frac{s-b}{g-s}\right\rfloor+1-n\right)." />
        <p>
          For the displayed rewards this is <Math tex="\max(0,60-n)" />. Accumulated
          evidence eventually makes one late loss too small to reverse the ranking.
        </p>
        <p>With a constant step size, the event instead moves the estimate to</p>
        <Math display tex="q_0=(1-\alpha)g+\alpha b," />
        <p>and after <Math tex="k" /> later ordinary outcomes,</p>
        <Math display tex="q_k=g-(g-q_0)(1-\alpha)^k." />
        <p>
          At <Math tex="\alpha=0.2" />, recovery always requires 12 risky visits,
          regardless of how much evidence preceded the event.
        </p>
        <p>
          While risky is non-greedy, its possible recovery visits over the remaining
          horizon are
        </p>
        <Math display tex="X_\tau=\sum_{t=\tau+1}^{T-1}\operatorname{Bernoulli}\!\left(\epsilon_t/2\right)." />
        <p>
          Because epsilon changes with time, <Math tex="X_\tau" /> is Poisson-binomial.
          For update rule <Math tex="u" />, the exact depth-one error probability is
        </p>
        <Math display tex="\Pr(\text{lock-in}\mid\tau,u)=\Pr(X_\tau<k_u)." />
        <p>
          The recovery counts are equal after 48 prior successes. Before that point,
          sample averaging is more vulnerable; afterward, constant alpha is more
          vulnerable. The ordering follows from the recovery thresholds and does not
          depend on the particular epsilon schedule. The probability levels do.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="exact-table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Exact depth-one lock-in probabilities" tabindex="0">
        <p class="table-label">EXACT DEPTH-ONE CALCULATION · b = −5</p>
        <table class="exact-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Event round</th><th scope="col">Sample average</th><th scope="col">Constant α</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>0</td><td><strong>1.000</strong></td><td><strong>0.013</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td>48</td><td><strong>0.473</strong></td><td><strong>0.473</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td>60</td><td><strong>0.000</strong></td><td><strong>0.692</strong></td></tr>
            <tr><td>100</td><td><strong>0.000</strong></td><td><strong>0.994</strong></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="article-section" aria-labelledby="depth-title">
      <div class="section-rail"><span>04</span><p>THE SECOND CLOCK</p></div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="depth-title">A terminal outcome must reach the choice.</h2>
        <p>
          One-step TD updates the risky chain online in forward order. In the adverse
          traversal, earlier links bootstrap from successors that have not yet received
          the bad target. Only the terminal predecessor changes immediately. Additional
          risky traversals are required for the shock to travel back to the start.
        </p>
        <p>
          This creates two clocks. The first governs propagation of the loss to the
          start decision. The second governs propagation of later ordinary outcomes that
          repair the estimate. Once the start becomes safe-greedy, the route traversals
          needed for repair arrive primarily through declining exploration.
        </p>
      </div>
      <figure class="wide-figure">
        <img
          src={study.results.figure}
          alt="Six panels compare sample-average and constant-step-size TD. With deeper routes, constant-step-size lock-in rises across most event times, while very late shocks fail to reach the start before training ends."
        />
        <figcaption class="figure-caption"><strong>Figure 1.</strong> Final probability of selecting the safe, suboptimal action after one forced terminal outcome. Columns vary route depth; rows repeat the study at bad rewards −5 and −4. Lines are paired estimates and ribbons are Wilson 95% intervals.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          At event round 48, depth one is the analytic crossover: both rules finish
          safe-greedy in 47.8% of paired simulations. At depth three, the corresponding
          probabilities are 0.0% for sample averaging and 99.7% for constant alpha. At
          depth six they are 0.0% and 100.0%.
        </p>
        <p>
          The nearby <Math tex="b=-4" /> condition reproduces the separation. At its
          depth-one crossover, round 39, the two rules both finish safe-greedy in 21.4%
          of simulations. At depth three the probabilities are 0.0% and 95.6%; at depth
          six, 0.0% and 99.99%.
        </p>
        <p>
          Depth also creates a late propagation blind spot. With constant alpha and
          depth six, an event at round 184 changes the final policy in 98.7% of runs.
          At round 188 it changes none: twelve remaining rounds are insufficient for
          the terminal target to reach the start. That zero is delayed credit, not recovery.
        </p>
        <p>
          A full-return update sends the terminal outcome to every risky state in the
          same traversal. Its curve is therefore identical to depth one at every tested
          route depth. The change in Figure 1 comes from one-step temporal credit
          propagation, not from adding inert states to the diagram.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="article-section" aria-labelledby="reading-title">
      <div class="section-rail"><span>05</span><p>THE READING</p></div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="reading-title">Robustness and responsiveness are the same lever.</h2>
        <p>
          Sample averaging gives each new outcome less weight as evidence accumulates.
          That protects a mature estimate from a late isolated shock. Across several TD
          links, the attenuation compounds: after enough prior traversals, the terminal
          disturbance never pushes the start value below the safe value.
        </p>
        <p>
          A constant step size preserves sensitivity to new outcomes. That is useful in
          a changing world, but here it preserves the size of the shock while the
          remaining exploration budget contracts. Route depth delays the policy change,
          then delays its repair even more.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is not evidence that sample averages are generally better. Their immunity
          to late evidence is also an inability to adapt quickly. Nor is constant alpha
          intrinsically defective: the finite-horizon failure depends on an annealed
          behavior policy that stops revisiting the demoted route.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="article-section" aria-labelledby="limits-title">
      <div class="section-rail"><span>06</span><p>LIMITS</p></div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="limits-title">A controlled mechanism, not a general theory.</h2>
        <p>
          The safe value is known and fixed. The pre-event history contains a calibrated
          number of successful risky traversals rather than a naturally generated
          trajectory. Rewards and transitions are otherwise deterministic. The risky
          chain has no decisions after the start and uses discount one.
        </p>
        <p>
          The results do not establish the same timing window for eligibility traces,
          replay, function approximation, recurrent agents, or deep RL. They also do not
          establish novelty. Early-outcome dependence and the hot-stove effect have an
          existing literature; deep RL has a separate, function-approximation form of
          <a href="https://proceedings.mlr.press/v162/nikishin22a.html">primacy bias</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The contribution of this note is deliberately smaller: an exact depth-one
          impulse response and a paired demonstration that one-step backup depth creates
          a propagation-and-recovery window, including a late horizon blind spot.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="article-section reproducibility" aria-labelledby="repro-title">
      <div class="section-rail"><span>07</span><p>REPRODUCIBILITY</p></div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="repro-title">One script and three compact artifacts.</h2>
        <p>
          The complete calculation and simulation run from one study-specific Python
          entry point. It does not use the laboratory's experiment protocol, a notebook,
          or raw Parquet shards. The published CSV contains every plotted estimate and
          confidence interval; the JSON file records the intervention, parameters,
          headline values, and deterministic propagation windows.
        </p>
        <p class="figure-downloads">
          <a href={study.results.data} download>Results (CSV)</a>
          <a href={study.results.analysis} download>Analysis record (JSON)</a>
          <a href={study.results.figure} download>Figure (SVG)</a>
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/pstarvaggi/independent-rl-lab/blob/main/experiments/stochastic_maze/run_bad_luck_timing.py">Open the analysis code <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <footer class="article-closing">
      <p>
        The next useful test is not a larger sweep. It is to remove the calibrated
        success history and ask whether the same timing window survives naturally
        generated rare outcomes.
      </p>
      <a class="text-link" href="/experiments/">All studies →</a>
    </footer>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
